# CLI experience migration

These changes are unreleased and target the next minor after v0.25.0; the
v0.25.0 release retains the previous contracts. Commands still declare `Args`,
`Options`, `meta`, and `execute`. Plugin authors and applications with custom
error handling should review the new invocation lifecycle before upgrading.

## Repeatable values and stdin

Array options now keep each CLI occurrence as one element. `--ac "a, b" --ac c`
produces `["a, b", "c"]`. If a list option needs `a,b` shorthand, declare
`meta.options.<name>.delimiter = ','`; empty segments are then rejected.
Free-text options should normally stay literal. [Args & options]($link.page('docs/args-options'))
has the exact syntax and edge cases.

To let a text option read a pipeline, set `meta.options.<name>.stdin = true`
on a `[]const u8` or `?[]const u8` field. Only a final, explicit CLI value of
`-` reads stdin. A default, environment, or config value of `-` stays literal.
The default input cap is 16 MiB, configurable with `stdin_max_bytes`.

## Plugin lifecycle and failure policy

The old `preExecute`, `postExecute`, and `onError` declarations need migration:

| Previous use | Current hook |
|---|---|
| Print help/version and stop | `handleInformation(context) !zcli.InvocationAction`: return `.complete` or `.proceed` |
| Load settings for option defaults | `loadConfig(context) !void`, then `applyConfigDefaults` |
| Authenticate, connect, or start a service | `prepare(context) !void`, after validation |
| Finish work after any stage | `onFinish(context, success) !void` |
| Attach a failure identity or message | `describeFailure(context, failure) !?zcli.failure.Description` |
| Render failure output | Plugin `renderFailure(context, failure) !bool` |

Raw argument rewriting remains in `preParse`, `transformArgs`, or `postParse`.
Help/version finish before config loading, stdin resolution, and operational
preparation. Errors from all fallible hooks enter the same failure path.
`onFinish` must tolerate incomplete setup; `deinitContextData` remains the
unconditional resource cleanup hook.

Applications can configure `.exit_codes = .{ .usage = 64,
.command_not_found = 64 }` in `zcli.generate`. A `failure_policy_module` may
export scoped `zcli.failure.ErrorRule` values to map command and plugin errors
to statuses, plus an application renderer. `context.fail` now records an
explanation and returns `error.CommandFailed`; use `context.failWith` to retain
a specific domain error. Normal errors should unwind through the invocation
engine. See [Error handling]($link.page('docs/errors')) and
[Build integration]($link.page('docs/build')).

`runInvocation` in `zcli_testing_unit` now tests the production routing,
parsing, plugin, stdin, and failure pipeline without exiting. `runCommand`
still tests a command body with typed values. See
[Testing]($link.page('docs/testing')).

## Shared imports and typed command settings

Plugins discovered from a project's `plugins_dir` receive its declared
`shared_modules`; remove manual `addImport` workarounds for those modules.
Dependency-provided plugins keep their own imports.

For declarative per-command settings, a plugin may declare a defaulted
`CommandConfig` struct and read `context.command_config.<plugin_id>` during
its hooks. A command overrides selected fields in `meta.plugins.<plugin_id>`.
Unknown plugin names and fields fail at compile time; group settings do not
cascade to children. Prefer lazy resource acquisition when a command simply
requests a client on demand. See [Plugins]($link.page('docs/plugins')).

## Editors and tables

An edit command can call `context.prompts().edit(config)` to open an editor
immediately on the controlling terminal, even with stdout redirected. Its
structured result is either `.edited` or `.failed`; failures can preserve a
recoverable scratch file. Programmatic `.argv` and quoted `$VISUAL`/`$EDITOR`
commands work without shell expansion. The existing `editor` question still
invites Enter by default and reads stdin off a terminal; `.immediate = true`
skips that invitation interactively. See [Interactive prompts]($link.page('docs/prompts')).

For a list command, `try context.table().print(columns, rows)` prints a
one-shot table. Terminal output fits the available width; redirected output
preserves full cells without color. See [Static tables]($link.page('docs/ui').unsafeRef('static-tables')).

The full migration notes and architecture decisions are also available in the
[repository documentation](https://github.com/ryanhair/zcli/blob/main/docs/CLI_MIGRATION.md).
